    "question": "1. What is signal integrity and how do you maintain it in high-speed digital designs?",
    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nSignal integrity is the ability of an electrical signal to travel from a transmitter to a receiver cleanly enough that the receiver samples the right logic level at the right clock edge—without reflections, crosstalk, overshoot, or ground bounce corrupting the waveform. In real post-silicon systems, signal integrity is inseparable from power integrity, because if the power delivery network drops during a heavy current transient, transistor switching slows down and noise margins shrink. At enterprise SoC on CoreCluster workload profiling, I reviewed schematics to locate VDD_CORE, VDD_CORE_M, current-sense points, and CLKOUT1, and used a 4-channel Keysight oscilloscope across High-Current Peak, Unzip, VEC_MEMCPY, and DSP_FFT to capture how 110 mV of supply droop correlated with current spikes and clock behavior.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nHigh-speed edges contain high-frequency harmonics (f_knee ≈ 0.5 / t_rise), so PCB traces behave as transmission lines rather than simple wires. Whenever a trace encounters an impedance discontinuity—such as a via stub, socket contactor, package ball, or missing return-plane reference—part of the wave reflects back (Γ = (Z_L − Z₀) / (Z_L + Z₀)), causing ringing and inter-symbol interference (ISI) that closes the data eye. Simultaneously, rapid current steps (dI/dt) across package and board inductance L create inductive voltage droop (V_L = L · dI/dt) on the supply rail. Maintaining SI/PI requires: (1) controlled trace impedance (typically 50 Ω single-ended, 85–100 Ω differential) with proper ODT/series termination; (2) unbroken ground reference planes so return current follows directly beneath the signal trace; (3) tight length matching and spacing (≥ 3W rule) to limit skew and crosstalk; and (4) low-inductance decoupling capacitors placed right at the SoC package.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Probe selection first: replace any long alligator ground lead with a < 5 mm spring-ground tip or active differential/power-rail probe so probe loop inductance doesn't create fake ringing.\n2. Measure rise/fall time, overshoot/undershoot, and eye width/height right at the receiver or nearest decoupling cap (not just at the VRM output).\n3. Check board termination and ODT/ZQCal settings (e.g., verify ZQCal isn't railed at 0x1ff or skewed like 0x101 vs peer lanes at 0x13D–0x142).\n4. Correlate SI noise against simultaneous rail droop (AC-coupled 10–50 mV/div scope channel on VDD/VDDQ) to separate PDN-induced jitter from trace reflection.",
