    "question": "2. Explain the difference between single-ended and differential signaling.",
    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nSingle-ended signaling sends one wire carrying the voltage relative to a shared ground plane, which makes it simple and pin-efficient but vulnerable to ground bounce and external noise. Differential signaling uses two tightly coupled complementary traces—positive and negative—and the receiver looks only at the voltage difference between them, so any noise coupled equally onto both wires cancels out. In my Flagship SoC Core work, I measured a single-ended clock routed out to the CLKOUT1 GPIO pin via SMA-to-BNC, configuring APC UART memory-mapped registers in Little Kernel and selecting DVFS0 via CPM UART. Firmware reported 4.112 GHz internally, while through an 80:1 divider (~51.4 MHz expected) the scope captured High-Current Peak excursions between 48.2 MHz and 54.1 MHz.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nIn single-ended interfaces (like standard GPIO, SPI, or legacy CMOS clocks), the receiver threshold sits halfway between VDDIO and GND; if return currents shift the local ground potential by 100 mV, the effective noise margin drops by 100 mV immediately. Differential interfaces (like PCIe, USB3, SerDes, and LPDDR5 CK/DQS strobes) reject common-mode noise because V_diff = V_P − V_N. They also emit far less EMI because equal and opposite currents generate canceling magnetic fields, allowing smaller voltage swings (hundreds of millivolts instead of 1.8 V/3.3 V) at multi-gigabit speeds. The main failure mode of differential pairs is intra-pair skew (P and N trace lengths mismatched), which converts differential energy into common-mode noise and shrinks eye width.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. On a single-ended clock/signal: check Vpp against VIH/VIL thresholds, duty cycle (target ~45–55%), edge monotonicity, and verify the divider ratio (e.g., 80:1 -> 4.112 GHz / 80 = 51.4 MHz) before calling a lower scope frequency a failure.\n2. On a differential pair: use a matched active differential probe across P and N close to the receiver, plus single-ended checks on P and N separately to measure common-mode offset and intra-pair skew.\n3. Verify AC coupling caps (e.g., 0.1 µF/0.22 µF on PCIe TX) are present, same value on P and N, and not tombstoned.\n4. Check differential termination (100 Ω across P/N or 50 Ω to VTT) for excessive reflections.",
