    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nI verify the power-clock-reset dependency chain using a single multi-channel oscilloscope power-up capture rather than checking pins one by one with a multimeter. Specifically, I confirm every power rail ramps monotonically without dipping, Power-Good stays low until rails settle, the reference clock is oscillating cleanly at target amplitude and frequency before reset releases, and reset holds low for at least the datasheet minimum pulse width.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nIf SYS_RESET# or PCIe PERST# de-asserts even a few microseconds before the core rail reaches Vmin or before the 100 MHz REFCLK / PLL locks, flip-flops exit reset with undefined states, state machines lock up, or PCIe LTSSM fails Detect/Polling immediately. Likewise, non-monotonic rail ramps (a dip during turn-on caused by inrush current into bulk capacitance) can cause internal power-on-reset (POR) circuits to double-pulse.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Capture a synchronized 4-channel oscilloscope & logic timing trace across Core VDD, IO/PLL Rail, AC-coupled REFCLK, and SYS_RESET# / PERST# (shown in the 4-channel waveform diagram above).\n2. Set Single-Sequence Rising Edge trigger on Ch1 (or Power-Enable) with 10–50 ms/div timebase to see the full sequence, then zoom to µs/div at the reset edge.\n3. Measure Δt from last rail reaching 95% nominal + stable REFCLK to reset rising edge; compare against datasheet t_POR / t_PERST.\n4. Check CPLD/TI UCD sequencer status registers over I2C/PMBus for any PG timeout or over-current fault flags.",
