    "question": "9. What are the key specs you consider when using an oscilloscope?",
    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nBefore trusting any waveform on an oscilloscope, I check four core specifications: analog bandwidth, real-time sample rate, probe loading/dynamic range with DC offset, and memory depth. If any one of those four is undersized for the signal I'm measuring, the scope will either attenuate real high-speed ringing or miss short transients entirely—just like slow telemetry tools miss nanosecond rail droop.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\n• Analog Bandwidth (−3 dB point): Must be at least 3–5× the highest fundamental frequency—or more accurately, governed by edge rise time: BW ≥ 0.5 / t_rise (10–90%). A 1 GHz scope distorts a 100 ps edge.\n• Sample Rate (GSa/s): Nyquist says > 2× BW, but for accurate waveform reconstruction without sin(x)/x interpolation artifacts on single-shot transients, you want ≥ 4–5× analog bandwidth.\n• Probe Bandwidth, Capacitance & DC Offset: A standard 10× passive probe with a 15 cm ground lead adds ~15 pF and > 100 nH of loop inductance—creating fake 100 MHz ringing! For mV ripple on a 1.2 V rail, use a 1:1 / low-attenuation power-rail probe with built-in DC offset so you can zoom to 10–50 mV/div and use the full ADC resolution.\n• Memory Depth: Sample Rate × Time Window = Memory Depth. Deep memory lets you capture a 10 ms workload transition while still sampling fast enough to resolve nanosecond droop.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Verify scope + probe combined bandwidth (system BW = 1 / √(1/BW_scope² + 1/BW_probe²)).\n2. Check input coupling & impedance: 50 Ω DC for high-speed clocks/SMA cables; AC coupling (or DC + offset on power-rail probe) at 10–50 mV/div for rail ripple/droop.\n3. Confirm probe attenuation factor in scope channel menu matches the physical probe (1× vs 10×) so voltage measurements aren't off by 10×.\n4. Check effective sample rate after setting horizontal timebase—make sure deep memory is enabled so sample rate doesn't silently drop.",
    "source": "https://www.keysight.com/us/en/lib/resources/application-notes.html",
