    "question": "10. How do you use a DMM to measure current on a board?",
    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nOn a high-current SoC or GPU validation board, I never break the main power rail to put a DMM in series, because the DMM's internal shunt and test leads add series resistance and inductance that will brown out the chip or blow the DMM fuse. Instead, I locate the precision current-sense resistor (R_sense) on the board schematic, measure the small DC voltage drop (V_sense) directly across its Kelvin sense pads with the DMM in DC Volts mode, and calculate current using Ohm's Law: I = V_sense / R_sense.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nPrecision board shunts are typically 1 mΩ to 10 mΩ (e.g., 5 mΩ). At 10 A load, V_sense is only 50 mV—so probing anywhere other than the dedicated Kelvin sense traces introduces copper-trace resistance error (V = I · R_trace) that can easily double your reading. If you are measuring a low-current auxiliary rail (< 500 mA) via an inline header/jumper where breaking the loop is explicitly designed in, you must check the DMM's fused port (mA vs 10 A), account for burden voltage (I × R_DMM_shunt), and never hot-switch ranges while current is flowing. Also remember a DMM integrates over tens of milliseconds—for fast dI/dt load steps, use a differential scope probe across R_sense or a clamp-on current probe.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Open the board schematic (e.g., Allegro) to find exact R_sense value, tolerance (e.g., 1% / 0.5%), and Kelvin sense test points.\n2. Zero/null DMM lead offset first (touch probes together in mV mode) because V_sense may only be a few millivolts.\n3. Measure V_sense differentially across the shunt pads (not referenced to board GND) and compute I = V_sense / R_sense.\n4. Cross-check calculated current against PMIC/VRM telemetry (I2C/PMBus READ_IOUT) to verify calibration.",
