    "question": "11. How would you validate a power rail with a DAQ setup?",
    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nTo validate a power rail with a Data Acquisition (DAQ) system, I wire differential analog input channels across the rail's Kelvin sense points near the SoC load (and across R_sense if logging current), verify common-mode voltage and grounding so I don't create a ground loop, and run a Python script that steps through idle, heavy load, and thermal corners while logging timestamped min, max, mean, and standard deviation against datasheet DC tolerance.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nA DAQ complements an oscilloscope: an oscilloscope captures nanosecond-to-microsecond AC transients, whereas a multi-channel DAQ (16–24 bit ADC) excels at long-duration, high-accuracy DC telemetry across 8–32 rails simultaneously over 24-hour thermal/Vmin soaks. Key electrical pitfalls when setting up a DAQ: (1) exceeding the DAQ's input common-mode voltage range (V_cm) when measuring a high-side shunt resistor on a 12 V rail; (2) ground loops when using single-ended DAQ inputs referenced to mains earth; and (3) aliasing if the rail has high-frequency switching ripple near the DAQ sample rate without hardware low-pass filtering.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Verify DAQ channel mode is Differential (not RSE/referenced single-ended) and within the DAQ's ±10 V / V_cm spec.\n2. Short each DAQ channel pair briefly at the bench to measure baseline noise floor and offset before connecting to the DUT.\n3. Cross-check the first DAQ reading against a calibrated handheld DMM at the exact same sense points.\n4. Log UTC timestamps alongside workload/thermal phase markers and flush CSV rows continuously so a 24-hour run never loses data.",
