    "question": "13. How do you validate an Ethernet PHY at board level?",
    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nI validate an Ethernet PHY in three layers from bottom up: first, electrical bring-up (rails, 25/50/125 MHz reference clock, reset release, and hardware strap pins); second, management & MAC-to-PHY link (reading PHY ID and link registers over MDIO/MDC and checking RGMII/SGMII/USXGMII timing); and third, live traffic & physical compliance (running internal/external loopback and iperf3 while monitoring ethtool -S for zero CRC/FCS errors).\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nMany board-level Ethernet failures happen in two classic places: (1) Strap pins sampled at reset de-assertion (determining PHY address, auto-negotiation, and RGMII internal clock delay—wrong pull-up/down values leave the PHY silent on MDIO or mis-timed); and (2) RGMII timing / MDI magnetics, where missing 2 ns TX/RX clock delay on RGMII causes packet loss at 1000 Mbps while 100 Mbps still works, or a mis-wired transformer center-tap bias breaks voltage-mode/current-mode MDI signaling. At system hardware team on Silicon One, checking L1/SerDes and FCS counters immediately separated MDIO/strap config errors from physical channel/SI loss.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Verify PHY power rails, crystal/REFCLK frequency & jitter, and reset pulse timing relative to clock stability.\n2. Read PHY ID registers (0x02/0x03) via MDIO (phytool or firmware CLI) to confirm MDIO/MDC communication and strap address.\n3. In Linux, run lspci | grep -i ethernet, ip link, ethtool eth0 (verify Speed: 1000Mb/s or 10G, Duplex: Full, Link detected: yes), and ethtool -S eth0 for RX/TX CRC, FCS, or symbol errors.\n4. Run PHY loopback + iperf3 stress across 100M/1G/10G; if 100M passes and 1G drops packets, check RGMII 2 ns TXC/RXC delay straps and MDI 100 Ω differential routing/magnetics.",
