    "question": "17. What's the typical DDR training sequence during boot?",
    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nAt cold power-on, external DRAM cannot hold data until the Memory Reference Code (MRC) or DRAM initialization firmware calibrates the PHY interface to the physical board traces. The sequence runs in four main stages: first, DRAM reset and ZQ impedance calibration (ZQCL) against an external precision resistor; second, Command/Address (CA) training relative to the clock; third, Write Leveling and Read/Write DQS-to-DQ 2D eye centering; and fourth, locking the calibrated parameters and running a quick BIST before unlocking the NoC and booting the OS.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nIn modern SoCs, early boot runs out of tiny internal SRAM (BL2), which fetches a signed DRAM init binary (such as signed_dram_init.bin / matched DRAM ID matched to the DRAM vendor ID). The training FSM executes: (1) ZQCal (PCODE PMOS pull-up / NCODE NMOS pull-down) against a 240 Ω reference resistor so driver impedance matches PCB trace Z₀ (~40–50 Ω); (2) CA Training so Read/Write/Precharge commands are sampled cleanly; (3) Write Leveling & DQS-DQ Shmoo, sweeping picosecond phase delays and millivolt Vref (1D/2D eye-margin shmoo) with checkerboard/PRBS patterns to map the 2D data eye; and (4) Eye Locking (Status 7 pass vs Status ff fail), which commits the DRAM address range to the NoC decoders and lets the CPU enable its MMU.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Inspect early UART/BL2 boot logs for did vendor match, training stage reached (ZQCal, CA, WrLvl, RdEye, WrEye), and final status (7 vs ff).\n2. Read per-channel ZQCal PU/PD codes—check for single-channel outliers (e.g., 0x101 vs 0x13D–0x142) or rail-ceiling saturation (0x1ff).\n3. Check VDD_MC and VDDQ_MEM rails during training—if an incompatible A0/B0 binary drives 0x1ff, current spikes can trip PMIC protection (PF State).\n4. Compare 2D eye-margin shmoo eye maps per byte-lane to see if a specific lane has collapsed width (timing) or height (Vref/noise).",
