    "question": "19. How do you check PCIe link speed and width in Linux?",
    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nI use lspci -vv -s <BDF> (or lspci -vv | grep -iE 'vga|3d|lnkcap|lnksta') to compare LnkCap—what the device and root port are capable of—against LnkSta—what speed and width they actually trained to, such as 16 GT/s Gen4 x16. I also run lspci -tv to see the PCIe switch/root-port tree and check dmesg for AER link-training errors.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nPCIe devices advertise their maximum capability in LnkCap (2.5 GT/s = Gen1, 5 GT/s = Gen2, 8 GT/s = Gen3, 16 GT/s = Gen4, 32 GT/s = Gen5) and negotiate down during LTSSM Configuration/Recovery if signal integrity, lane continuity, or REFCLK fails—resulting in a lower LnkSta speed or narrower lane width (e.g., x8 instead of x16). One critical subtlety in Linux: PCIe ASPM (Active State Power Management) intentionally drops link speed to Gen1 when the GPU/endpoint is idle to save power, so always check LnkSta while the device is active!\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Find device BDF: lspci | grep -iE 'vga|3d|gpu' (or target vendor) and view topology with lspci -tv.\n2. Compare capabilities vs live link: sudo lspci -vv -s <BDF> | grep -E 'LnkCap:|LnkSta:|UESta:|CESta:'.\n3. For Python/Bash automation, read /sys/bus/pci/devices/0000:<BDF>/current_link_speed and current_link_width under active workload (not idle).\n4. Check dmesg -T | grep -iE 'pcie|aer' for link-down, bandwidth-downgrade, or Replay/Receiver error bursts.",
