    "question": "21. A new board powers up but there's no boot log. What are your step-by-step debug actions?",
    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nI never assume a dead chip or corrupted BIOS right away—I debug strictly from the outside in. First, I verify the host UART connection (lsusb, /dev/serial/by-id/, baud rate, TX/RX wiring, and no competing terminal reader). Second, I measure all board power rails and Power-Good pins against the schematic. Third, I check reference clocks and reset de-assertion on an oscilloscope. Only after power, clocks, and reset are verified clean do I check SPI flash / JTAG and firmware SHA256.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nA USB-to-UART bridge stays enumerated in Linux even when the target SoC is completely unpowered or stuck in reset. on EVB bring-up boards, 'no serial sign-on' almost always traced back to one of four root causes: an unprogrammed or faulted TI UCD power sequencer, a missing reference clock, SYS_RESET# held low, or a wrongly flashed/unseated SPI BIOS chip (or A0 vs B0 signed image mismatch).\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Linux host check: lsusb, ls -l /dev/serial/by-id/, sudo dmesg --follow—confirm adapter is present and no second logger holds the port; scope the UART TX pin from the SoC to see if any bytes are toggling.\n2. Power rails & sequencer: probe every VR output with a DMM against schematic tolerances; check TI UCD / PMIC PG flags.\n3. Clock & Reset: probe crystal/100 MHz REFCLK and SYS_RESET# on a 4-channel scope during power-on.\n4. Firmware & JTAG: check sha256sum of the flashed binary, inspect socket/flash seating, and test JTAG IDCODE scan.",
