    "question": "23. How would you troubleshoot an intermittent PCIe Gen4 link training issue?",
    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nFirst I preserve the failing dmesg and lspci -vv AER logs without rebooting. Second, I isolate whether the issue follows the DUT, the riser/slot, or the bench fixture via controlled A/B swaps and microscope inspection of contacts/pins. Third, I check 100 MHz REFCLK quality and PERST# timing, and test locking max link speed down to Gen3/Gen2—if Gen3 passes 100% while Gen4 (16 GT/s) fails intermittently, we've proved it's a high-frequency equalization or signal-integrity margin issue rather than a hard digital bug.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nAt Gen4 (16 GT/s, 8 GHz Nyquist), channel insertion loss is roughly double Gen3, so link training relies heavily on TX preset optimization (Phase 2/3 Equalization in LTSSM Recovery) and RX CTLE/DFE adaptation. Marginal socket contact resistance, bent pins, dirty gold fingers, riser impedance discontinuities, or excessive REFCLK SSC/jitter eat up the remaining eye margin, causing intermittent fallback to Gen3/Gen1 or narrow width (x8/x4) and Replay/Receiver errors.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Capture sudo lspci -vv -s <BDF> (LnkCap, LnkSta, AER Correctable/Uncorrectable) and dmesg -T | grep -iE 'pcie|aer'.\n2. Inspect PCIe gold fingers, riser connector, and SoC socket under a microscope for bent pins, debris, or flux residue.\n3. Perform controlled A/B swap (suspect DUT in known-good slot/riser; known-good DUT in suspect slot/riser).\n4. Force Gen3 speed (setpci or BIOS/root-port link cap) and run PCIe Lane Margining / SerDes eye scan to pinpoint which physical lane(s) are marginal.",
