    "question": "24. DDR validation fails under thermal stress but not at room temp — how would you debug?",
    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nFirst I keep the failing assembly intact and separate whether the failure happens at hot, at cold, or during the thermal ramp itself. Next, I align on-die thermal sensors against chamber/T-Case readings so I know the true silicon temperature, inspect ZQCal calibration registers and interrupt status at the failing corner, and check whether retraining DDR at that target temperature recovers margin—which tells us whether boot-time room-temp training isn't tracking thermal drift.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nHot and cold failures have opposite physics: at hot (85–105 °C), capacitor leakage doubles roughly every 10 °C and transistor threshold shifts slow switching, shrinking tREFI retention and setup margins. At cold (−10 °C to −25 °C), interconnect resistance drops and driver behavior shifts; in my enterprise SoC memtester dvfs investigation, a chamber dip to −25.56 °C (−8 °C on-die mem sensor / −3 °C aux sensor on-die) drove the ZQCal FSM to saturate at 0x1ff across all channels, destroying impedance matching and triggering GMC/GSLC interrupts (0x400/0x100) and Failed at PF State: 13.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Separate steady-soak thermal fails from ramp-rate fails; record both on-die sensors (on-die thermal sensors) and T-Case/chamber RTDs with aligned UTC timestamps.\n2. Inspect ZQCal PU/PD codes at room vs failing temp (watch for saturation at 0x1ff or single-channel outliers like 0x101).\n3. Compare 2D eye-margin shmoo eye shmoo at 25 °C vs failing temp and test cold-booting/retraining directly at the failing temperature.\n4. Check VDD_MC and VDDQ_MEM rails at temperature for VRM thermal drift or increased dI/dt droop.",
