    "question": "26. A system crashes randomly — what logs or test methods would you use to isolate the root cause?",
    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nBecause a random crash often wipes local volatile state, I start by preserving every post-mortem artifact—journalctl -b -1, /var/log/kern.log, pstore/kdump, BMC/SEL logs, system GPU telemetry CLI GPU fault event/ECC status, and continuous external UART logs. Next, I strip the system to a minimal configuration and run isolated stress loops per subsystem (CPU, DRAM, PCIe, Thermal) while logging rail voltage and T-Case, so I can turn a random crash into a deterministic, reproducible test case.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nRandom crashes usually fall into four buckets: (1) Power/Brownout (PMIC UVLO or dI/dt droop causing silent hard reset with zero OS kernel panic); (2) Thermal runaway/throttling; (3) Memory/Cache corruption (MCE, SError, ECC DBE); or (4) PCIe/Driver deadlock (AER Fatal, GPU fatal GPU bus-drop event fallen off bus). Knowing whether the OS printed a panic/GPU fault event vs died instantly without a single UART byte immediately splits hardware power/reset faults from software/bus faults.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Recover previous boot logs: journalctl -b -1, /var/log/kern.log, /sys/fs/pstore/*, and external bench tail -F $AP_LOG.\n2. Check dmesg -T | grep -iE 'mce|aer|GPU fault event|ecc|panic|segfault|watchdog' and BMC/IPMI SEL (ipmitool sel list).\n3. Isolate subsystems one by one: run memtester alone, then GPU compute alone, then PCIe I/O alone, each with rail/thermal logging.\n4. If hard-hanging with zero OS logs, set a scope trigger on SYS_RESET# / PWRGOOD falling edge + core VDD to catch the hardware trip.",
