    "question": "27. Write a Python script to: Read voltage/current values from a DAQ, log them with timestamps, and plot power over time.",
    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nI structure the script with pyvisa (or vendor DAQ SDK) inside a try/finally block so the VISA session always closes cleanly even on error, log UTC ISO timestamps with f.flush() after every measurement so crashes never lose data, compute instantaneous power P = V × I, and generate a clean matplotlib plot.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nIn 24-hour SLT bench automation, two bugs destroy lab scripts: buffered file I/O (leaving a 0-byte CSV if the host reboots) and leaked VISA/instrument handles (locking out the next test). Calling f.flush() per row, setting an explicit daq.timeout, converting SCPI strings immediately with float(), and closing resources in finally makes the logger production-safe. On headless Linux servers without a display, matplotlib.use('Agg') ensures plotting never crashes on missing X11.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Run rm.list_resources() and daq.query('*IDN?') first to verify VISA driver and instrument address.\n2. Verify channel numbers (@101 voltage, @102 current/shunt) and scaling factor (1 / R_sense if reading shunt mV).\n3. Test with samples=5 first, inspect power_log.csv against a handheld DMM, and confirm power_plot.png renders cleanly.",
