    "question": "28. You're given a CSV log with performance and power — write a function to flag outliers.",
    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nI combine hard engineering spec limits (such as datasheet max TDP or minimum throughput) with statistical outlier detection using pandas, checking for NaN or dropped samples as well as z-score or Median Absolute Deviation (MAD) excursions so both hard spec violations and subtle distribution shifts get flagged.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nStandard z-score (|x − mean| / std > 3) works well for roughly normal telemetry, but one massive 100× spike in a log can inflate mean and std enough to mask smaller real outliers! Using MAD (median(|x − median(x)|)) alongside hard spec bounds makes outlier detection immune to extreme spikes, and checking .isna() catches instrument timeouts that logged blank cells.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Inspect df.dtypes and df.isna().sum() first—string error tokens like 'ERR' in a CSV turn numeric columns into object dtype.\n2. Guard against zero standard deviation (std == 0) on constant idle rails so division never produces NaN.\n3. Unit-test with synthetic clean rows, injected spikes, and blank rows before running across fleet logs.",
