    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nIn validation engineering, teams build and maintain Python, Bash, and PowerShell automation frameworks for 24-hour DRAM qualification, automated Vmin characterization across CPU, GPU, TPU, DPU, nTPU, and GMC, and precision thermal head dual-head thermal control. The framework handled pre-flight serial/SSH and thermal checks, head-level locking so dual thermal heads never collided on commands, deterministic preconditions (LK mode, DRAM init, PF settings), structured CSV/UART logging, and automated JMP/JSL heatmap generation.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nGood SLT automation separates configuration (SKU tables, fused-voltage targets, PVT corners), transport/instrument drivers (VISA, serial /dev/serial/by-id/, precision thermal head TCP/serial with explicit command-acknowledgement parsing and head-level mutexes), test execution, and post-processing (1D/2D eye-margin shmoo log parsing and 10% Vmin screening comparisons). Most importantly, every run wraps hardware control in try/finally safety hooks so a DUT hang or script interrupt automatically returns the thermal head to 25 °C idle and releases locks.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Pre-flight check: verify /dev/serial/by-id/ symlink, SSH reachability, thermal head acknowledgement, and T-Case within ±1 °C of target before starting.\n2. Dry-run / unit-test parsers with pytest + mocked instrument/serial responses before deploying to 24-hour hardware benches.\n3. Inspect per-run artifact folder (raw_uart.log, thermal.csv, summary.csv) to verify zero mixed logs across retries.",
