    "question": "32. A board from another team shows inconsistent behavior. How do you handle the handoff?",
    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nI treat it as a strict chain-of-custody handoff: before changing a single jumper or cable, I record the board ID, SoC/DRAM SKU, rework history, firmware SHA256, and their exact failing command and logs. Next, I perform an unpowered physical inspection under a microscope for bent pins, damaged caps, or rework bridges. Then I reproduce their exact baseline test on my bench before running any new isolation experiments.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nCross-team 'ghost bugs' frequently come from hidden environment differences: a different power supply current limit, a missing jumper/strap, an older A0 vs B0 firmware image, or mechanical damage during transport. Reproducing the sender's exact condition first—and keeping separate log files for baseline vs post-inspection—ensures you never confuse a bench mismatch with a silicon defect.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Document serial numbers, BOM/rework tags, jumper positions, and sha256sum of every firmware/script file handed off.\n2. Unpowered microscope check: inspect socket pins, BGA/rework areas, sense resistors, and connectors.\n3. Run the sender's exact command on their exact config first to establish baseline reproduction rate before A/B swapping.",
