    "topic": "POST SILICON QA",
    "question": "33. You're asked to validate 50 boards under thermal stress in 2 days. How do you plan and scale?",
    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nI combine fast upfront triage, parallel station execution, and full automation. First, I run a quick 5-minute room-temp smoke screen (power, UART, lspci, basic DDR) so dead or mis-assembled boards don't waste hours inside thermal chambers. Second, I calculate station capacity and parallelize across all available thermal heads/chambers with barcode/serial ID tracking. Third, I run headless Python/Bash automation with head-level locking and live CSV pass/fail dashboards so failing boards are flagged immediately.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nWith 50 boards in 48 hours, manual clicking or sequential debugging will fail the schedule. You have to optimize throughput = (parallel stations × uptime) / cycle_time: prioritize the highest-risk PVT corners (hot soak + cold soak) over redundant intermediate steps, stage spare cables/adapters/fixtures so one worn contactor doesn't stall a rig, and automate log parsing so pass/fail decisions take seconds instead of manual spreadsheet review.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Smoke-screen all 50 boards at room temp first (lspci, system GPU telemetry CLI, UART boot, basic memtester) to filter DOA hardware.\n2. Verify station-to-station correlation with one golden reference board across all chambers/heads before loading the 50-board lot.\n3. Enforce /dev/serial/by-id/, per-DUT log folders, and automated CSV summary scripts checking PASS/FAIL, T-Case soak stability, and error counts.",
