    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nDuring a tight memory qualification schedule schedule, an automated memtester dvfs run failed immediately on Loop 1 at cold temperature with unwanted GMC and GSLC interrupts. Under deadline pressure, it's tempting to start swapping boards and cables blindly—but I kept the failing assembly intact, verified the Linux USB-UART connection and firmware checksums first, aligned the on-die sensors against the chamber dip, and isolated the exact PF_high failing state and 0x1ff ZQCal saturation so the engineering team had clean, actionable root-cause evidence.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nWhen schedules are tight, changing multiple variables at once actually slows down resolution because you destroy the failing state and can't tell which change mattered. By following Verify → Observe → Log → Isolate → Escalate, we proved the -25.56 °C chamber dip caused ZQCal FSM ceiling saturation (0x1ff) and 0x400/0x100 interrupts at PF_high rather than random bit-cell damage.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Preserve raw logs (AP_LOG, chamber CSV, dmesg) and freeze hardware state before touching anything.\n2. Verify host UART (lsusb, /dev/serial/by-id/, dmesg --follow) and sha256sum of firmware.\n3. Reconstruct exact failing timeline (1m22s Loop 1 -> 1m27s 0x400/0x100 interrupts) and share a concise 1-page evidence packet.",
