    "question": "36. What's one lesson you learned from a failed board bring-up?",
    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nThe biggest lesson I learned from bring-up on reference EVB bring-up boards is: never assume a silent serial console means a software or BIOS failure, and never change two variables at once. Checking power rails, power sequencing, reference clocks, and reset release on the schematic before reflashing firmware saves hours—and keeping a clean baseline log before reseating or reworking prevents losing the original failure signature.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nFirmware is completely passive until hardware satisfies power, clock, and reset. Reflashing five BIOS builds onto a board whose TI UCD sequencer hasn't released PWRGOOD only adds confusion and risks damaging the socket or SPI flash pads. Turning every failed bring-up into a repeatable checklist (SOP/BKM) protects the whole lab.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Verify power rails -> clocks -> reset -> JTAG/SPI activity in strict order before reflashing.\n2. Change only one variable per retest and save separate logs for each attempt.\n3. Document the root cause and fix in the team's BKM/SOP.",
