    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nI structure the USB validation suite around five core test cases—enumeration speed check (lsusb -t 5G/10G vs 480M), sequential read/write throughput & data integrity (fio/dd + checksum), suspend/resume recovery, repeated hotplug/port-reset cycles, and kernel/xHCI error-counter verification (dmesg)—wrapping each test in try/finally logging so failures capture dmesg and usbmon traces automatically.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nA USB 3.0 port can silently drop to USB 2.0 (480 Mbps) or rack up xHCI transfer retries while a simple file copy still technically finishes. Validating both negotiated SuperSpeed link rate and zero kernel/CRC errors catches marginal signal integrity, bad AC coupling caps, or retimer bugs that functional-only scripts miss.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Verify lsusb -t reports 5000M or 10000M (not 480M) before and after every suspend/reset test.\n2. Check dmesg -T | grep -iE 'usb|xhci' after every iteration for silent link resets or descriptor read retries.\n3. Verify data integrity with sha256sum on read-back files, not just throughput MB/s.",
