    "question": "Panelist 1 Follow-up Q2: Which project are you most proud of and why?",
    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nI am most proud of two complementary projects at enterprise SoC: first, building the precision thermal head dual-head thermal automation with setpoint/T-Case verification, command acknowledgements, and head-level locking; and second, my CoreCluster post-silicon workload profiling where I showed that existing slow on-chip PM telemetry telemetry missed sub-microsecond droop and captured the exact 110 mV High-Current Peak undershoot and CLKOUT1 excursions on a Keysight oscilloscope.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nIn automated 24-hour thermal/Vmin testing, dual-head controllers can suffer command collisions or begin stressing a DUT before die T-Case actually stabilizes, creating false silicon failures. Adding mutex head-level locking, explicit command-ack parsing, and T-Case soak verification eliminated bench-induced noise. On the electrical side, pairing slow on-chip PM telemetry with a 4-channel Keysight scope (spring-ground probing on VDD_CORE, VDD_CORE_M, shunt current, and 80:1 divided CLKOUT1) turned vague 'heavy workload' concerns into exact millivolt and megahertz margin data.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Verify head-level lock release inside try/finally so a crashed test never leaves a thermal head locked or stuck at −25 °C / +105 °C.\n2. Cross-check scope probe ground length (< 5 mm) and divider ratio (80:1 -> 4.112 GHz / 80 = 51.4 MHz) before presenting waveform deltas.\n2. Measure and log against constraint (Throughput = (Parallel_Stations * Uptime) / Cycle_Time) while keeping raw unmodified captures intact.",
