    "question": "Panelist 2 (ASIC Validation) Q3: How do you differentiate between ATE testing and System Level Testing (SLT)?",
    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nATE—like structural ATE with production ATE software used in high-volume production silicon test—uses deterministic high-speed pin electronics to screen structural faults such as stuck-at scan, transition delay, MBIST, IDDQ, and DC/AC parametrics in just seconds per part. System Level Testing puts that packaged SoC into a real motherboard or platform booting actual firmware and Linux, running concurrent CPU, GPU, PCIe, and DRAM workloads over minutes or hours to catch functional, thermal, and multi-domain timing escapes that ATE patterns cannot reproduce.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nATE tests blocks largely in isolation under controlled tester power supplies and rigid vector timing. Real systems introduce: (1) simultaneous switching noise across multiple domains (CPU + GPU + NoC + DRAM) causing first-droop IR collapse; (2) dynamic DVFS/PF-state transitions and real PMIC settling loops; (3) full protocol state machines (PCIe Gen4/5 LTSSM equalization, DDR MRC 2D training); and (4) self-heating thermal gradients. Because SLT takes minutes per unit while ATE takes seconds, SLT's mission is both final quality gating and feeding escape signatures back to DFT/ATE to tighten structural screens.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Compare ATE bin/Vmin logs against SLT failing PF state, temperature, and workload to see if the escape is speed-path, PDN-droop, or protocol-specific.\n2. Check if raising VDD by +25–50 mV on the SLT bench makes the ATE-pass chip pass SLT—confirming a timing/IR-drop escape.\n2. Measure and log against constraint (ATE Pass + SLT Fail = Real-Workload Interaction (PDN dI/dt, IO PHY, Multi-Clock)) while keeping raw unmodified captures intact.",
