    "question": "Panelist 2 Follow-up Q4: If a chip passes ATE but fails SLT, what would be your first debug step?",
    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nBefore blaming the silicon, I freeze the exact failing SLT state and rule out the bench environment first: socket/contactor seating, board power rails, thermal T-Case stability, firmware/BIOS SHA256, and host serial/PCIe links. Once the failure is confirmed reproducible, I run a controlled A/B swap—putting the suspect chip into a second known-good SLT board and a golden reference chip into the suspect board—to prove whether the defect follows the chip or the fixture.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nA huge percentage of 'ATE-pass / SLT-fail' units in real labs turn out to be worn socket contactors, bent pins, wrong firmware stepping (A0 vs B0), or unstabilized T-Case soak. Once A/B swapping proves the failure follows the silicon, you characterize the failing boundary (voltage shmoo, frequency shmoo, temperature sensitivity, and failing IP block like DDR vs PCIe) to give ASIC/DFT teams an exact reproduction recipe.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Preserve failing UART/dmesg/AER/thermal logs and firmware sha256sum before removing the chip.\n2. Inspect socket contacts under a microscope and reseat once to test repeatability.\n3. Execute A/B cross-swap with a golden reference DUT on a verified reference board.\n4. On confirmed silicon fails, sweep VDD (+/−50 mV) and lock DVFS/PCIe Gen speed to isolate timing vs functional defect.",
