    "question": "Panelist 3 (DFT Engineer) Q5: How familiar are you with scan chains or BIST structures in post-silicon validation?",
    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nI have worked with DFT and BIST flows both on production structural ATE across production ATE and bench environments (running structural scan, HDBI, ICDC, and memory BIST) and in my university ASIC verification coursework. Scan chains stitch internal flip-flops into serial shift registers so ATPG patterns can shift in test states and shift out captured responses, while MBIST and LBIST are on-die self-test engines that test dense SRAM arrays and logic at-speed without external pin bottlenecks.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nExternal I/O pins are far too few and too slow to directly observe millions of internal SoC flip-flops or gigabits of on-die SRAM. Scan solves internal controllability and observability for stuck-at and transition-delay faults, while MBIST runs deterministic algorithmic sequences (like March C-, checkerboard, address-complement) across every SRAM bank, row, and column—often feeding redundancy/repair fuses. In post-silicon SLT, DFT hooks remain invaluable: scan-dumps capture internal architectural state after a system hang, and BIST/PRBS engines isolate failing SRAM banks or SerDes lanes.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Check if the failing SLT subsystem exposes on-die MBIST status/repair registers or SerDes PRBS/loopback counters.\n2. On a silent hang, coordinate with DFT to trigger JTAG scan-dump / crash-dump before cycling board power.\n3. Compare failing SLT bit/lane addresses against ATE scan/MBIST datalog bins for that die ECID.",
