    "question": "Panelist 3 Follow-up Q6: How would you leverage DFT to debug a failing subsystem?",
    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nIf an SLT workload fails deterministically on a specific subsystem—say an L2 cache, memory controller, or PCIe/SerDes lane—I partner with DFT to switch that block into its internal test hooks: running localized MBIST with fail-bitmap readout for memory arrays, enabling internal/external SerDes PRBS loopback to separate PHY analog margins from MAC/protocol logic, or pulling a JTAG scan-dump on hang to see which FSM state wedged.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nFunctional OS workloads exercise millions of transistors at once, making root-cause localization noisy. DFT modes strip away OS/driver/NoC variables: if SerDes PRBS-31 passes in internal digital loopback but fails in external pad loopback, the fault is strictly in the analog front-end, package ball, socket, or PCB trace rather than digital RTL. Similarly, an MBIST fail bitmap pinpoints exact failing row/column/bank.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Read chip ECID/fuse ID so DFT can pull wafer/ATE wafer-sort history.\n2. Run internal digital loopback vs external pad/fixture loopback (or MBIST at failing voltage/temp corner).\n3. Sweep VDD ±50 mV during the DFT hook to determine whether it is a hard physical defect or a Vmin timing margin wall.",
