    "question": "Panelist 4 (Hardware Engineer) Q7: Suppose DDR initialization is failing intermittently. What debug methods would you apply?",
    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nI apply a strict single-variable isolation sequence: first preserve the failing UART/MRC logs, temperature, and PF state; second verify the Linux host USB-UART capture and firmware sha256sum so we don't chase a dropped logger or an A0/B0 image mismatch; third inspect ZQCal impedance codes, VDD_MC/VDDQ_MEM power sequencing, and socket seating; and fourth run controlled A/B swaps between the suspect assembly and a verified reference.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nIntermittent DDR init failures happen when a training margin sits right on the pass/fail cliff—so small shifts in contact resistance, VDDQ_MEM ripple, temperature, or ZQCal (PCODE/NCODE) push Status 7 (pass) into Status ff (fail). During qualification, inspecting ZQCal values caught a single-channel 0x101 outlier (62–65 steps below peer lanes at 0x13D–0x142) that caused eye collapse, as well as cold-induced 0x1ff ZQCal saturation at PF_high. Keeping cold boot, warm reset, and sleep-resume separate also reveals whether stale controller state is leaking across resets.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Host & FW check: lsusb, ls -l /dev/serial/by-id/, sudo dmesg --follow, and sha256sum -c ./SHA256SUMS (confirm right did & stepping).\n2. Inspect MRC/BL2 log for exact failing training stage (ZQCal, CA, WrLvl, DQS-DQ) and per-channel ZQCal PU/PD values (0x101 outlier or 0x1ff rail).\n3. Probe VDD_MC and VDDQ_MEM during init with AC-coupled scope (10–50 mV/div, < 5 mm ground spring) for droop or phase noise.\n4. Inspect socket/contactor under microscope, reseat original assembly, repeat ≥ 10 cold boots, then A/B swap with golden reference.",
