    "topic": "SYSTEM LEVEL QA",
    "question": "Panelist 5 Follow-up Q10: How would you extend it to store results in CSV with timestamps?",
    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nI parse any embedded timestamp from the start of the log line (supporting both ISO-8601 and dmesg [seconds.usec] formats, with a UTC ingestion fallback), include dut_id and line_no, and stream matches directly into csv.DictWriter with newline='' and periodic flush() so downstream JMP or pandas pipelines can join error events against thermal and voltage CSVs.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nStreaming directly to csv.DictWriter keeps memory constant even if a failing test emits 500,000 error lines, while preserving log_timestamp lets engineers align an AER or MEMTESTER ERROR event to the exact second a thermal chamber crossed −25.56 °C or a VRM dipped.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Verify newline='' in open() so Windows/Excel/JMP doesn't see blank double-spaced rows.\n2. Verify CSV opens cleanly in pandas.read_csv() and JMP even when error messages contain commas or quotes.\n2. Measure and log against constraint (Throughput = (Parallel_Stations * Uptime) / Cycle_Time) while keeping raw unmodified captures intact.",
