    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nAs an SLT engineer, I don't guess or make that call in isolation—my role is to deliver crisp, quantitative evidence so engineering leadership can weigh risk accurately: (1) severity and customer impact (silent data corruption or hard hang vs benign corner); (2) failure rate across skewed process lots and PVT corners; (3) whether a firmware, MRC training, or DVFS/Vmin workaround cleanly eliminates it and at what power/performance/yield cost; and (4) whether a metal-only ECO suffices versus a full-mask respin.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nA respin costs millions and months of schedule, while shipping an unmitigated silent-data-corruption (SDC) bug can trigger massive field recalls. Validating a proposed workaround requires proving it doesn't merely hide the failure by skipping the stressed PF state or blowing TDP/yield budgets—for example, raising Vmin by +50 mV might pass functionally but fail 10% fused-voltage screening or thermal spec.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Quantify exact failure rate across multiple units, skewed corners (SS/TT/FF), and full temperature range.\n2. Test any proposed FW/DVFS/MRC workaround across all PVT corners and check power/clock/yield impact.\n3. Present confirmed data (never speculation) in a Go/No-Go risk table.",
