    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nRather than calling ax.clear() on every sample—which leaks memory and slows down over hours—I pre-create the plot line once, store a rolling window in collections.deque(maxlen=window), and update the line coordinates in place with line.set_data().\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nIn long thermal soak tests, unbounded lists and repeated ax.clear() calls bog down the UI thread. On headless Linux SLT servers without X11, switching to matplotlib.use('Agg') and saving periodic PNG snapshots avoids display crashes. Specifically for t.842ee7x0mlgm-1 (Q2: Live temperature plotting using matplotlib), combining physical probing (Throughput = (Parallel_Stations * Uptime) / Cycle_Time) with reproducible logging ensures single-variable root-cause proof across voltage, frequency, and thermal corners.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Verify DISPLAY or use Agg backend if running over headless SSH.\n2. Bound deque(maxlen=...) so 24-hour runs keep constant RAM.\n2. Measure and log against constraint (Throughput = (Parallel_Stations * Uptime) / Cycle_Time) while keeping raw unmodified captures intact.",
