    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\ncopy.copy() duplicates only the outer container while sharing references to nested mutable objects, whereas copy.deepcopy() recursively clones every nested list or dictionary. In SLT bench configs, deepcopy prevents one DUT's voltage override from accidentally mutating the shared baseline template.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nIf base_cfg = {'dvfs': {'v_mv': 750}}, doing dut2 = base_cfg.copy() and setting dut2['dvfs']['v_mv'] = 700 silently overwrites base_cfg['dvfs']['v_mv'] too! Specifically for t.842ee7x0mlgm-10 (Question3: Describe the difference between deepcopy and shallow copy in Python.), combining physical probing (Throughput = (Parallel_Stations * Uptime) / Cycle_Time) with reproducible logging ensures single-variable root-cause proof across voltage, frequency, and thermal corners.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Assert id(dut2['dvfs']) != id(base_cfg['dvfs']) in config-loader unit tests.\n2. Measure and log against constraint (Throughput = (Parallel_Stations * Uptime) / Cycle_Time) while keeping raw unmodified captures intact.\n3. Execute A/B or fault-injection verification ( Lock baseline -> preserve immutable raw evidence -> isolate one variable per step) to confirm zero silent regressions.",
