    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nBefore touching a board or socket, I confirm the exact socket architecture—LGA, PGA, BGA, or Test/Burn-in socket—against the schematic and BOM, enforce full ESD controls (grounded mat, tested wrist strap, ionizer), and stage a PCB support jig, calibrated hot-air or reflow station, solder paste, no-clean flux, IPA, and a stereo microscope.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nPre-production silicon and fine-pitch sockets are vulnerable to latent ESD damage and mechanical warpage if the PCB isn't supported flat on a rigid jig during placement and reflow. Specifically for t.hxhq6pol1183-0 (1. How do you prepare the workspace and identify the socket before assembly?), combining physical probing (A/B Matrix: (Good DUT + Suspect Socket) vs (Suspect DUT + Good Socket)) with reproducible logging ensures single-variable root-cause proof across voltage, frequency, and thermal corners.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Test wrist strap & ESD mat ground continuity.\n2. Verify socket part number, pitch, and PCB footprint revision match the BOM.\n2. Measure and log against constraint (A/B Matrix: (Good DUT + Suspect Socket) vs (Suspect DUT + Good Socket)) while keeping raw unmodified captures intact.",
