    "question": "2. How do you prepare PCB pads and apply solder paste for BGA/LGA sockets?",
    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nI clean every pad thoroughly with isopropyl alcohol and lint-free swabs, inspect pad coplanarity and solder mask under the microscope, apply a thin uniform coat of flux, and use a laser-cut stainless-steel stencil to deposit consistent solder paste across the pad array.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nExcess paste causes hidden solder bridges underneath the socket body, while insufficient paste causes cold/open joints—both of which create intermittent high-speed lane or power-delivery failures. Specifically for t.hxhq6pol1183-1 (2. How do you prepare PCB pads and apply solder paste for BGA/LGA sockets?), combining physical probing (A/B Matrix: (Good DUT + Suspect Socket) vs (Suspect DUT + Good Socket)) with reproducible logging ensures single-variable root-cause proof across voltage, frequency, and thermal corners.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Inspect stenciled paste bricks under the microscope for smearing or bridged apertures before placing the socket.\n2. Measure and log against constraint (A/B Matrix: (Good DUT + Suspect Socket) vs (Suspect DUT + Good Socket)) while keeping raw unmodified captures intact.\n3. Execute A/B or fault-injection verification ( Freeze baseline -> inspect ESD/paste/pins -> execute 2x2 A/B swap before rework) to confirm zero silent regressions.",
