    "question": "7. How do you isolate a bad socket/contactor from a bad silicon chip in SLT?",
    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nOn complex server and ASIC bench setups, worn contactors and bent pins were a frequent source of false silicon fails. I inspect the socket under a microscope for bent pins or flux/debris, clean and reseat the original DUT once, and then perform an A/B cross-swap—testing the suspect DUT in a second verified station and a golden reference DUT in the suspect socket.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nIf multiple unrelated chips fail the same pin/lane/channel inside one socket while passing in another station, the fault is fixture wear rather than silicon. Specifically for t.hxhq6pol1183-6 (7. How do you isolate a bad socket/contactor from a bad silicon chip in SLT?), combining physical probing (ATE Pass + SLT Fail = Real-Workload Interaction (PDN dI/dt, IO PHY, Multi-Clock)) with reproducible logging ensures single-variable root-cause proof across voltage, frequency, and thermal corners.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Inspect contactors under high magnification and log insertion-cycle count.\n2. Run A/B cross-swap with a golden chip before binning any DUT as a silicon failure.\n2. Measure and log against constraint (ATE Pass + SLT Fail = Real-Workload Interaction (PDN dI/dt, IO PHY, Multi-Clock)) while keeping raw unmodified captures intact.",
