    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nI use dmesg -T for timestamped kernel ring-buffer messages, filtering with dmesg | grep -iE 'vga|3d|gpu' for GPU_RM/GPU fault event errors and dmesg | grep -i pcie for AER or link-down events. For systemd services and previous-boot crashes, I use journalctl -xe, journalctl -b for the current boot, and journalctl -b -1 to see what happened right before an unexpected reboot or hang.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\ndmesg reads /dev/kmsg, where the Linux kernel and device drivers log hardware interrupts, PCIe Advanced Error Reporting (AER), IOMMU faults, and Silicon SLT GPU_RM: GPU fault event error codes (such as fatal GPU bus-drop event: GPU has fallen off the bus or GPU fault event 48: Double Bit ECC Error). Because dmesg resets on reboot, journalctl -b -1 (when persistent journald storage is enabled) is essential for post-mortem analysis after a hard lockup or power loss.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Run dmesg -T | grep -iE 'gpu_drv|gpu_rm|GPU fault event|pcie|aer' and record the exact timestamp of the first error.\n2. Check journalctl -b -1 -p err if the host crashed or rebooted during the previous test run.\n3. Export both logs cleanly (dmesg -T > dmesg.log and journalctl -xe > journal.log) before power-cycling.",
