    "question": "Linux Q7: How do you monitor system thermals, fans, and power supplies on a Linux test station?",
    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nI use sensors (from lm-sensors, initialized once with sudo sensors-detect) to read motherboard, CPU, VRM, and fan telemetry in real time, alongside system GPU telemetry CLI for GPU die and memory temperatures. On systems with battery or power-supply reporting, upower -d shows supply status. To watch thermals live during a run, I combine watch -n 1 sensors or watch -n 1 system GPU telemetry CLI.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nThermal throttling happens when junction or board temperatures exceed firmware thresholds, causing clocks and voltage to step down to protect silicon—which can masquerade as a performance regression or intermittent timing failure. In my enterprise SoC bench work, I also saw how host-level or chamber-level sensors can differ significantly from on-die silicon sensors, so correlating sensors / system GPU telemetry CLI die temps with bench airflow/chiller state prevents misdiagnosing a cooling failure as bad silicon.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Run sensors to verify CPU, ambient, and fan RPMs are within expected bench limits.\n2. Run system GPU telemetry CLI -q -d TEMPERATURE,POWER to check GPU slowdown/shutdown thresholds and active throttle reasons.\n3. Log watch -n 1 or CSV sensor polling alongside the workload so thermal ramps are timestamped.",
