    "question": "Linux Q8: How do you check USB debug adapters, block storage, NVMe drives, and network interfaces on a Linux bench host?",
    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nFor USB-to-UART debug cables and fixtures I check lsusb and /dev/serial/by-id/. For storage I run lsblk to see mount topology and nvme list plus dmesg | grep -i nvme for NVMe health. For networking—critical when PXE-booting DUTs or streaming telemetry—I check ip a, ip link, lspci | grep -i ethernet, and ethtool eth0 to verify link speed and duplex.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nIn an SLT or lab rack, a 'hung DUT' or 'failed log transfer' often traces back to a flaky USB-UART bridge, a dropped 10GbE/1GbE management NIC (ethtool showing Link detected: no or negotiated down to 100Mb/s), or a full/read-only NVMe scratch drive. Using /dev/serial/by-id/ avoids /dev/ttyUSB0 renumbering after USB resets, and ethtool verifies physical layer negotiation directly at the NIC driver.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Run lsusb and ls -l /dev/serial/by-id/ (plus dmesg -T | grep -i usb) if console/fixture communication drops.\n2. Run ip link and sudo ethtool <iface> to confirm physical carrier, speed, and duplex on management/PXE NICs.\n3. Run nvme list and dmesg | grep -iE 'nvme|blk_update_request' to rule out storage I/O errors.",
    "source": "https://www.ti.com/lit/an/slva689/slva689.pdf",
