    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nFirst I check the Linux bench host using lsusb, ls -l /dev/serial/by-id/, and sudo dmesg --follow in a separate terminal. I look for USB disconnects, bus resets, or a tty device disappearing at the exact timestamp the log froze. If a cable or hub is flaky, I swap only that link, confirm the console path with readlink -f \"$AP_PORT\", and retest the original assembly.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nA USB-to-UART bridge can stay enumerated in lsusb even when the target SoC is unpowered or wedged, so seeing /dev/serial/by-id/ only proves the host-to-adapter link—not that the target is alive. Equally important, you should never launch a second serial terminal on a port already owned by the logger, because competing readers steal bytes and corrupt log parsing. And fixing a USB cable does not justify swapping boards; you retest the exact original assembly first.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Run ls -l /dev/serial/by-id/ (or /dev/serial/by-path/) and sudo dmesg --follow for USB disconnect/re-enumeration timestamps.\n2. Check lsof \"$AP_PORT\" to verify no competing process is reading the UART port.\n3. After replacing a suspect cable/hub, re-run the exact diagnostic on the original board and classify the earlier incomplete log as invalid.",
