    "question": "HLD Q3 (Step 4): How do you rule out a firmware or image mismatch before blaming the silicon or socket?",
    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nI verify both file integrity and hardware compatibility. On the Linux host I run sha256sum \"$IMAGE\" or sha256sum -c ./SHA256SUMS, and then check the package manifest and target boot banner to confirm the binary matches the exact silicon revision (like A0 vs. B0), board config, and DRAM vendor/topology. For hardware isolation, I lock one verified compatible firmware package and never change firmware at the same time as reseating hardware.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nA SHA-256 match only proves the file wasn't corrupted on disk—it does not prove it is the right binary for that SKU or silicon stepping. For instance, loading an A0-compiled DRAM init binary onto B0 silicon produces initialization/training failures that look electrical. Also, a known-good reference unit with a different DRAM SKU must use its own matching supported firmware rather than forcing identical filenames.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Run sha256sum -c ./SHA256SUMS on the host provisioning directory.\n2. Compare the target's boot/BL1/BL2 banner (chip rev, DRAM vendor/capacity) against the firmware release manifest.\n3. If a mismatch is found, document it, flash the correct package, and establish a fresh baseline on the original assembly.",
