    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nOnly after removing power and visually inspecting power rails, UART wiring, socket alignment, basket seating, retention hardware, and pins for contamination or damage. If there's any sign of a short or mechanical hazard, I fix that first. Otherwise, I keep variables isolated and run the documented cold power-cycle procedure while capturing boot logs from cycle zero.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nCold power-on, warm reset, and sleep-resume leave completely different amounts of residual state inside the memory controller, PHY PLLs, and DRAM mode registers, so they must never be conflated. Likewise, combining cleaning, reseating, firmware changes, and voltage margining in a single step makes it impossible to know which action actually cured the symptom.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Power off and inspect socket pins, basket alignment, retention torque/latch, and power/UART headers.\n2. Perform a clean cold power-cycle while recording UART from initial boot (BL1/BL2/dram_init).\n3. Record whether the failure reproduces deterministically from cold boot or depends on prior state.",
