    "question": "HLD Q5 (Step 7): If socket seating is suspected, how do you test that hypothesis cleanly without fooling yourself?",
    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nAfter preserving logs, I power down, inspect/clean/reseat the original DRAM and SoC in the original board and socket using the exact alignment and retention spec—keeping firmware, command, and temperature unchanged. After power-on, I verify /dev/serial/by-id/ and a clean boot log, then rerun the original test multiple times. A single pass isn't enough to declare an intermittent seating issue solved.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nThere is no Linux command that proves every DRAM ball or socket pin is making low-resistance contact; lsusb and readlink only confirm the host UART interface is back. Real proof of seating recovery comes from repeating initialization, ZQ calibration values, functional memtester loops, and electrical eye/margin measurements across multiple runs.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Re-verify ls -l /dev/serial/by-id/ and readlink -f \"$AP_PORT\" after power-on.\n2. Compare ZQ calibration codes and training eye margins before vs. after reseat on the exact same unit.\n3. Run multiple consecutive loops/reboots before attributing recovery to seating.",
