    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nFirst I inspect the raw target console output directly—if the DUT actually passed or reported something different from what the dashboard parsed, it's an automation/parser bug. If the raw DUT fails, I A/B compare the suspect firmware against the previous approved baseline release on the exact same hardware, fixture, temperature, and boot flow.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nEven when a newer firmware change makes a failing test pass, you cannot sign it off blindly—it might have fixed a timing table or calibration sequence, or it might have silently skipped a failing PF state or relaxed a margin! You must verify that the exact same operating states, frequencies, voltages, and margins were actually exercised.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Diff the raw UART log against the automation pass/fail parser output to rule out script bugs.\n2. A/B test baseline FW vs. suspect FW on identical hardware/temperature.\n3. Compare actual exercised PF states, clock frequencies, and shmoo margins between passing and failing FW.",
