    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nmemtester is the target memory diagnostic; dvfs exercises dynamic voltage and frequency scaling states; pattern [0,8,10,16] selects specific data stimulus IDs defined by the firmware; loops 50 sets the repetition count; and size 16 MB sets the test allocation size across ranks. I monitor live output with tail -F \"$AP_LOG\" and filter failures with grep -nE across loop, PF state, error, and ZQCal lines.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nA common trap is guessing what 0,8,10,16 mean without checking the firmware's pattern table—they are stimulus pattern IDs, not voltage states. Isolating which pattern fails guides physical debug: checkerboard exposes adjacent-line crosstalk/coupling, solid 1s/0s expose DC power droop or stuck faults, and address patterns expose decode/aliasing issues. Likewise, you must verify actual rank/cache mapping before claiming full DRAM coverage.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Keep separate log files per run and follow live with tail -F \"$AP_LOG\".\n2. Run grep -nE 'Loop count number|Failed at PF State|MEMTESTER ERROR|Pass count|Fail count|ZQCal' \"$AP_LOG\".\n3. Look up the exact firmware pattern-ID table for [0,8,10,16] and check buffer allocation across Rank 0 / Rank 1.",
