    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nIn that captured trace, Loop 1 started at 1m22s, printed temperature and five PF_high reports, logged ZQCal and interrupts, and at 1m27s exited with an unwanted GMC interrupt, an unwanted GSLC interrupt, 0 passes, and 2 failures—so it never completed 50 loops. To debug it, I test PF_high steady-state versus transitions into PF_high, and I align on-die sensors (on-die mem_sensor: -8 C, aux_sensor: -3 C) against chamber logs (-25.6C) using verified timestamps rather than assuming they are the same point.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nSeeing five PF_high print lines does not mean five separate data errors—the two failure counts correspond to the two rejected GMC/GSLC interrupt conditions (which must be confirmed in diagnostic source). Separating fixed-PF_high operation from DVFS transitions tells you whether voltage settling/clock-switch sequencing is failing versus steady-state electrical margin. And because chamber air (-25.6C) and on-die sensors (-8C/-3C) sit at different physical locations, appended chamber readings cannot be blindly treated as die temperature.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Check timestamps of Loop 1 start (1m22s) through exit (1m27s) to verify actual executed loop count.\n2. Compare fixed-state PF_high execution against dynamic DVFS transitions into/out of PF_high.\n3. Correlate on-die thermal sensors on-die sensors with chamber thermocouple timestamps using verified run boundaries.",
