    "question": "Comm Q3: Describe how you work in a team and communicate with design, firmware, or senior engineers when escalating a bug.",
    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nI treat escalation as handing my teammates a complete, reproducible puzzle—never just 'it failed.' Before pinging firmware or silicon designers, I run A-B-A checks, verify the firmware SHA-256 and hardware config, and package the raw dmesg/UART logs, scope screenshots, and exact reproduction command. That respects everyone's time and builds trust across lab technicians, firmware engineers, and silicon architects.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nCross-functional SLT teams move fast under tape-out and production schedules. When a technician or engineer escalates cleanly—separating observed facts from hypotheses—designers can act in minutes instead of asking five rounds of follow-up questions.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Confirm the bug is reproducible (or document exact intermittent rate, e.g., 2/39 runs).\n2. Bundle logs, HW/FW versions, and scope waveforms into one clean ticket.\n3. Clearly separate observed telemetry from your working hypothesis.",
