    "question": "Comm Q4: How do you handle shifting priorities, unexpected hardware bottlenecks, or changing test plans (Flexibility)?",
    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nIn bring-up and SLT labs, priorities change whenever a new silicon stepping arrives or a hot customer-blocking bug appears. I stay calm, finish preserving the current station's state so work isn't lost, confirm the new priority and acceptance criteria with the lead, and pivot cleanly. I also document where I paused so anyone—or I—can resume the earlier test seamlessly.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nLab teams need engineers who don't get rattled when a chamber goes down, a fixture breaks, or a priority shift happens mid-day. Staying methodical under pressure protects expensive silicon and prevents half-finished experiments from polluting test data.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Checkpoint current logs and label hardware state before pivoting tasks.\n2. Clarify scope, deadline, and pass/fail criteria for the new urgent task.\n3. Share a concise handoff note if another teammate picks up the paused bench.",
