    "id": "t.puny9iguska8-0",
    "topic": "Fundamental Knowledge Q&A — Translated & Expanded",
    "question": "Fund Q1: What is the fundamental relationship between charge, capacitance, and voltage in a DRAM cell?",
    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nCharge equals capacitance times voltage (Q = C × V), where Q is in coulombs, C is in farads, and V is in volts. A 1T1C DRAM cell stores a bit as charge on a tiny capacitor. When the wordline turns on the access transistor during a read, that stored charge shares onto a much larger precharged bitline, creating a small voltage shift that the sense amplifier detects and restores.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nBecause DRAM storage cells are microscopic (tens of femtofarads), charge Q leaks over milliseconds through junction and subthreshold leakage—which is why DRAM requires periodic refresh (tREFI) and why reads are destructive until the sense amplifier drives the bitline back to full rail. Any drop in V_cell from leakage or insufficient write-recovery time directly shrinks the charge available for sensing.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. If bit-flips disappear when refresh interval (tREFI) is shortened or temperature is lowered, suspect retention/leakage decay.\n2. Check tWR (write recovery) and supply voltage margins if cells fail right after writes.\n2. Measure and log against constraint (tRC >= tRAS + tRP  |  Q = C_cell * V  |  DeltaV_BL = (VDD/2)*[C_cell/(C_cell+C_BL)]) while keeping raw unmodified captures intact.",
    "source": "https://www.jedec.org/standards-documents",
