    "question": "Fund Q3: What is the relationship between tRC, tRAS, and tRP in DRAM row timing, and why does it matter?",
    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nRow-cycle time tRC must be at least tRAS plus tRP. tRAS is the minimum row-active time—how long the wordline must stay open so the sense amplifiers finish restoring charge into the cell. tRP is the minimum row-precharge time needed to close the row and equalize the bitlines back to VDD/2 before the next activation.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nFor example, if tRAS = 32 ns and tRP = 14 ns, then tRC must be at least 46 ns (exact numbers come from the JEDEC/device datasheet for the active speed bin). Violating tRAS cuts off charge restoration before the cell reaches full voltage, causing weak-cell read failures later; violating tRP leaves bitlines unequally precharged, shrinking sensing margin on the next ACTIVATE.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Dump the memory controller timing registers for the active speed bin and compare tRAS, tRP, and tRC against the DRAM datasheet.\n2. Test with relaxed tRP/tRAS (or lower frequency bin) to see if bank-thrashing errors disappear.\n2. Measure and log against constraint (tRC >= tRAS + tRP  |  Q = C_cell * V  |  DeltaV_BL = (VDD/2)*[C_cell/(C_cell+C_BL)]) while keeping raw unmodified captures intact.",
