    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nVoltage droop from fast current steps is governed primarily by package and PCB PDN inductance multiplied by the current slew rate (V_L = L × dI/dt), plus resistive IR drop. Even a few hundred picohenries of loop inductance multiplied by a steep dI/dt creates tens of millivolts of instantaneous droop before the VRM can even respond.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nA VRM's control loop operates in tens to hundreds of kilohertz (microseconds), whereas silicon current steps happen in nanoseconds (dI/dt in A/ns). Local on-die and package decoupling capacitors must supply that fast charge via I_C = C × (dV/dt) while package/via inductance L opposes sudden current change. On an oscilloscope, this shows up as a sharp negative spike followed by damped ringing.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Probe the rail AC-coupled (10–50 mV/div, full BW) with a < 5 mm ground spring right at the decoupling cap or sense point.\n2. Trigger on the negative droop edge and correlate its timing against workload bursts or ZQCal commands.\n2. Measure and log against constraint (P_total = (alpha * C * V^2 * f) + V * I_leak(T)) while keeping raw unmodified captures intact.",
