    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nThe RC time constant τ = R × C tells me how fast a node charges or discharges through resistance and parasitic capacitance—after one τ it reaches ~63%, and ~5τ to settle. Meanwhile, I_C = C × (dV/dt) tells me how much current a driver or supply must deliver to slew a capacitive load. If a rail ramps too slowly or a bus eye closes horizontally, excess C or series R is usually the culprit.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nOn a high-speed interface or I2C/reset line, excessive trace/pin capacitance or a wrong pull-up resistor inflates τ, causing edges to miss setup/hold windows or power-good timeouts. On power rails, ramping dV/dt too fast into huge bulk capacitance draws high inrush current, which can trip over-current protection (OCP) during power-on.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Measure 10%–90% rise/fall time on the oscilloscope and compare against 2.2 × R × C expected from schematic values.\n2. On power-up OCP trips, measure dV/dt ramp rate and calculate inrush current from total board capacitance.\n2. Measure and log against constraint (P_total = (alpha * C * V^2 * f) + V * I_leak(T)) while keeping raw unmodified captures intact.",
