    "question": "Fund Q6: What causes ringing on a power rail or clock/data trace, and how does the LC resonant frequency equation help you find the root cause?",
    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nRinging happens when parasitic inductance L and decoupling/load capacitance C form an underdamped tank circuit that oscillates at its natural LC resonant frequency after a fast switching edge. Measuring that ringing frequency f0 on an oscilloscope lets you immediately deduce whether the resonance comes from on-die/package parasitics (hundreds of MHz) or board/VRM inductance and bulk caps (kHz to low MHz).\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nEvery PDN has parallel anti-resonance peaks where PCB/package inductance resonates with decoupling capacitors. Also, a sloppy oscilloscope measurement with a long 15 cm alligator ground lead adds ~100+ nH of probe-loop inductance, creating fake ringing around 50–150 MHz with the probe tip capacitance—which is why verifying with a short ground spring is mandatory before blaming the board.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Measure the ringing period T on the scope (f0 = 1/T) using a short < 5 mm ground spring (never a long ground clip).\n2. Re-measure with a second probe location to confirm the frequency is real board/package resonance, not probe-loop inductance.\n2. Measure and log against constraint (P_total = (alpha * C * V^2 * f) + V * I_leak(T)) while keeping raw unmodified captures intact.",
