    "question": "Fund Q7: Explain the transmission-line reflection coefficient Γ and what happens at an open, short, or matched load.",
    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nThe reflection coefficient Γ equals (Z_L − Z0) divided by (Z_L + Z0), giving the fraction of a signal wave reflected back from a load impedance Z_L on a trace of characteristic impedance Z0. When Z_L matches Z0, Γ = 0 (no reflection). At an open circuit, Γ = +1 (full positive reflection, doubling voltage). At a short, Γ = −1 (full inverted reflection).\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nAt high frequencies (PCIe, DDR/LPDDR DQ/CK), PCB traces behave as transmission lines rather than lumped wires. Any impedance discontinuity—wrong ODT (On-Die Termination), missing termination resistor, via stub, or bad socket pin—creates non-zero Γ. Reflected energy bounces back and forth, causing overshoot, undershoot, non-monotonic edges (ledges), and inter-symbol interference (ISI) that closes the data eye.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Look for stepped/ledge edges or severe overshoot/undershoot on the scope at the receiver.\n2. Verify ODT/ZQ calibration settings (PCODE/NCODE) and trace termination resistors against Z0 (e.g., 50 Ω single-ended, 85–100 Ω differential).\n2. Measure and log against constraint (Gamma_L = (Z_L - Z0) / (Z_L + Z0)  |  V_refl = Gamma_L * V_inc) while keeping raw unmodified captures intact.",
    "source": "https://www.ti.com/lit/an/snla026a/snla026a.pdf",
