    "question": "How do you stream a large log in Python?",
    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nNever load a multi-gigabyte SLT soak log into RAM with f.read(); iterate lazily over the file handle line by line so memory stays constant (O(1)) at a few kilobytes regardless of file size.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nPython file objects implement an iterator protocol backed by an 8 KB buffered stream reader, yielding one newline-delimited string per step instead of allocating an entire list of strings in heap memory. Using errors='replace' prevents UnicodeDecodeError when UART noise injects partial UTF-8 bytes during brownouts, and pre-compiling regex patterns outside the loop avoids per-line DFA setup cost across millions of lines.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Open the UART log with open(path, 'r', encoding='utf-8', errors='replace') to survive garbled reset bytes.\n2. Stream lines via `for lineno, line in enumerate(f, 1):` instead of read() or readlines().\n3. Track line numbers and a small collections.deque(maxlen=5) context window before each error match.\n4. Benchmark peak RSS with tracemalloc on a synthetic 500 MB log to confirm flat O(1) memory.",
    "source": "https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/inputoutput.html#reading-and-writing-files",
