    "question": "Why use try/except around instrument reads?",
    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nBench instruments frequently drop VISA sockets, hit measurement timeouts on noisy rails, or return non-numeric status strings like '9.9E+37'—so unguarded reads crash the entire overnight rack run.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nOver GPIB, USBTMC, or TCP/IP VISA, electrical ESD events, bus contention, or slow trigger arms raise pyvisa.errors.VisaIOError before a voltage reading finishes. Wrapping reads in targeted `try/except (VisaIOError, ValueError)` lets the harness clear the instrument error queue (`*CLS`), log exact diagnostic context, safely retry bounded idempotent queries, and abort gracefully rather than leaving supplies powered.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Catch specific exceptions (VisaIOError, ValueError, TimeoutError) — never bare `except:` which hides Ctrl-C.\n2. Issue `inst.clear()` or `*CLS` after a VISA timeout so stale buffered bytes don't poison the next query.\n3. Reject sentinel out-of-range floats (>1e30 from DMM/DAQ overloads) before writing telemetry CSVs.\n4. Re-raise fatal connection loss after N bounded retries so the outer `finally` powers down safely.",
