    "question": "What is the purpose of a finally block?",
    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nA `finally` block runs unconditionally whether a test passes, raises a Python exception, or gets Ctrl-C interrupted—making it essential to turn off power supplies, return thermal heads to 25 C, and close VISA handles.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nLeaving a high-current programmable PSU enabled or holding a thermal forcing head at 105 C when a validation script crashes can overheat uncooled silicon or wedge USB/GPIB locks for the next job. Pairing `try/finally` with `contextlib.ExitStack` guarantees teardown runs in reverse acquisition order, though sudden OS power loss still demands a hardware watchdog.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Wrap PSU output enable, thermal setpoint change, and VISA open inside `try ... finally` or context managers.\n2. Inside `finally:`, isolate each cleanup step with its own inner `try/except` so a failed DMM close doesn't skip PSU shutdown.\n3. Restore safe idle states in hardware order: cut stress workload -> set thermal 25 C -> output OFF -> close handles.\n4. Also configure a hardware PSU over-temperature / watchdog timer for AC power loss (where Python cannot run).",
    "source": "https://docs.python.org/3/library/contextlib.html#contextlib.ExitStack",
