    "question": "What is the difference between shallow and deep copying?",
    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\n`copy.copy()` duplicates only the top-level container while sharing inner nested dicts or lists by reference; `copy.deepcopy()` recursively clones every nested level so modifying one test config never mutates the golden template.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nIn validation harnesses, engineers often load one golden station dict (`{'rails': {'vdd': 0.85, 'limits': [0.80, 0.90]}}`) and spawn per-corner configs using `cfg.copy()`. Because `cfg['rails']` still points to the exact same inner dictionary object in memory, lowering `vdd` to 0.72 V for the Vmin corner silently corrupts all subsequent nominal runs!\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Never reuse mutable default dicts/lists directly across parameterized corner runs.\n2. Use `copy.deepcopy(base_cfg)` when cloning nested rail thresholds, channel tables, or board maps.\n3. Verify object identity in tests using `assert clone['rails'] is not base_cfg['rails']`.\n4. For immutable shared lookup tables, prefer tuples or MappingProxyType to fail fast on accidental writes.",
    "source": "https://docs.python.org/3/library/copy.html",
