    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nUse threads (`ThreadPoolExecutor`) for I/O-bound orchestration—talking concurrently to 8 bench chambers, UARTs, and PSUs—and processes (`ProcessPoolExecutor`) for CPU-bound analysis like multi-million-sample FFTs or eye-shmoo curve fitting.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nCPython's Global Interpreter Lock (GIL) allows only one thread to execute Python bytecode at a time, so threads give zero speedup on pure-Python CPU loops. However, threads release the GIL during OS sleep, serial reads, SSH, and VISA socket waits—making threads ideal for parallel bench stations while processes use true multi-core parallelism (unless using NumPy C-releasing array ops).\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Profile with cProfile/perf_counter to confirm whether bottleneck is I/O wait (UART/VISA) or CPU math (FFT/CSV).\n2. Use `ThreadPoolExecutor` with thread-safe per-station queues for parallel I/O bench control.\n3. Use `ProcessPoolExecutor` under `if __name__ == '__main__':` for heavy multi-core log parsing or DFT/DSP.\n4. Keep separate output directories per worker so logs never interleave.",
