    "question": "How do you run a CLI test from Python safely?",
    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nAlways invoke external CLI tests using `subprocess.run(['cmd', arg], shell=False, timeout=..., capture_output=True, text=True)` so spaces/metacharacters cannot inject shell commands and hung tests never stall forever.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nPassing a raw string with `shell=True` lets filenames containing spaces, semicolons, or `; rm` trigger shell injection or subtle quoting breakage. Passing an argument list with `shell=False`, enforcing a finite `timeout`, capturing both `stdout`/`stderr`, and archiving the exact return code makes external runner calls deterministic and debuggable.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Pass commands as a list of strings (`['lspci', '-vv', '-s', bdf]`) with `shell=False`.\n2. Always specify a realistic `timeout=` so a frozen DUT CLI raises `subprocess.TimeoutExpired` instead of hanging.\n3. Use `capture_output=True, text=True` and archive both stdout and stderr to the run folder.\n4. Check BOTH `res.returncode` AND parsed stdout pass/fail criteria.",
    "source": "https://docs.python.org/3/library/subprocess.html#subprocess.run",
