    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nParse timestamped UART lines into timezone-aware `datetime` objects via `fromisoformat()` or compiled regex, normalize to UTC, and align by `abs(t_dut - t_chamber)` against chamber telemetry before claiming thermal correlation.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nTarget consoles often log boot-relative uptime (`[  14.230]`), local wall clock without timezone, or truncated millisecond strings while chamber loggers run UTC. Comparing mismatched clocks causes fake 1-hour DST or boot-offset shifts (mistaking a chamber ramp for a soaked corner failure).\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Force UTC ISO-8601 (`2026-11-15T09:12:04.125Z`) on both host UART recorder and thermal controller.\n2. Capture host-side timestamps at UART receipt IF the DUT prints only relative boot seconds (`[ 12.40s]`).\n3. Parse timestamps with a compiled regex + `datetime.fromisoformat()` rather than fragile string slicing.\n4. Align streams using bounded nearest-neighbor tolerance (`abs(delta) <= 2s`) and flag unsynced clocks.",
