    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nConfigure pyserial with a finite read `timeout` (e.g., `timeout=1.0`), accumulate partial chunks cleanly across timeout boundaries, and flush input/output buffers before resetting a DUT so incomplete prompts never deadlock the script.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nBy default (`timeout=None`), `ser.readline()` blocks forever if the target SoC hangs mid-line without sending a trailing `\\n`—stalling automated bench suites indefinitely! Worse, calling `readline()` with a short timeout can return a half-received fragment on timeout, so robust drivers buffer incoming fragments in a deadline loop until the expected token or overall step deadline expires.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Open serial ports with explicit non-blocking finite timeout (`serial.Serial(port, 115200, timeout=0.5)`).\n2. Loop against a monotonic `time.monotonic() + deadline` buffer when waiting for multi-line boot banners.\n3. Never treat a single partial `readline()` empty timeout as an immediate fatal crash—keep reading until deadline.\n4. Close or release ports cleanly on reset so USB-UART Linux driver re-enumeration doesn't leave stale `/dev/ttyUSB*` FDs.",
    "source": "https://pyserial.readthedocs.io/en/latest/pyserial_api.html#serial.Serial",
