    "question": "How do you test code without a real instrument?",
    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nInject a dependency Protocol/Fake instrument or `unittest.mock` object into your validation code so tests run deterministically in CI in milliseconds without touching physical GPIB hardware.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nTightly coupling validation logic to `pyvisa.ResourceManager()` makes unit testing impossible at your desk and hides edge-case bugs (like negative shunts, timeouts, or NAN rows) until expensive overnight lab time. Defining a tiny `MeterProtocol` with real SCPI in production and a lightweight deterministic fake in `pytest` verifies every parser, retry, and limit branch offline.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Inject `daq` or `serial` objects via function/class arguments instead of instantiating globals inside logic.\n2. Build a small fake/mock class implementing `.query()` returning clean, noisy, and timeout sequences.\n3. Assert edge cases (`VisaIOError`, NaN strings, finally PSU shutoff on crash) using `pytest` before deploying to benches.\n4. Run an explicit mocked vs hardware-in-loop tag so offline test reports never impersonate real silicon passes.",
    "source": "https://docs.python.org/3/library/unittest.mock.html",
