    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nCombine deterministic spec limits (e.g. `VDD outside [0.75, 0.95] V`) with robust Median/MAD outlier detection rather than mean/std z-scores alone—because one massive DAQ spike inflates `mean` and `std` enough to hide real smaller anomalies!\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nIn skewed or spike-contaminated hardware telemetry, a single 1000x DMM range glitch shifts the mean and blows up standard deviation, causing genuine 80 mV droops to drop below `|z| > 3`. Using Median Absolute Deviation (`MAD = median(|x - median|)`) keeps breakdown tolerance up to 50% outliers, while checking `MAD == 0` guards flat constant rails against division by zero.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Apply physical electrical spec bounds first (`v_min_spec`, `v_max_spec`) so real out-of-spec rails always flag regardless of baseline noise.\n2. Compute `med = statistics.median(vals)` and `mad = statistics.median(abs(x - med))` for robust statistical outliers.\n3. Guard `if mad == 0:` cleanly (common on quantized low-rate DAC/ADC channels).\n4. Plot flagged outliers directly onto the time series to confirm real transient vs measurement artifact.",
