    "question": "How do you use logging levels in an automated test?",
    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nConfigure Python's `logging` module with dual handlers: concise `INFO` messages on console stdout and full millisecond-stamped `DEBUG` register/SCPI traffic persisted to a rotating log file inside each test run folder.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nScattering bare `print()` statements makes 24-hour multi-station triage miserable: debug chatter floods terminals while zero timestamps survive if SSH disconnects. Structured logging levels (`DEBUG` SCPI commands, `INFO` phase boundaries, `WARNING` recoverable retries, `ERROR`/`CRITICAL` hardware aborts) let engineers replay exact command timing post-mortem without rerunning with edits.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Create a per-run logger writing `DEBUG+` with UTC ISO timestamps to `<run_dir>/bench_debug.log`.\n2. Attach a clean `StreamHandler` at `INFO+` so operators watch test phase progress cleanly.\n3. Log hardware transitions (`PF state`, thermal target, SCPI retries) at the moment executed.\n4. Never put `basicConfig` deep inside reusable library modules—configure handlers at the CLI entrypoint only.",
