    "question": "Why use a virtual environment for test scripts?",
    "answer": "WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nA Python virtual environment (`python3 -m venv .venv`) paired with a pinned `requirements.txt` / lockfile guarantees every lab bench runs the exact same `pyvisa`, `pyserial`, and `numpy` versions—never polluting the OS Python or breaking overnight updates.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nShared multi-user bench PCs frequently accumulate conflicting global pip installs: Station 1 runs `pandas 1.5` while Station 4 has `pandas 2.2`, causing API deprecation crashes mid-sweep! Isolating automation per project in a virtual environment with pinned versions (`==`) makes test deployments reproducible across stations and CI containers.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Never `sudo pip install` into the Linux system Python used by OS utilities (`apt`, `firewalld`).\n2. Create an isolated `.venv` per harness repo (`python3 -m venv .venv && .venv/bin/pip install -r requirements.txt`).\n3. Record exact `pip freeze > requirements-lock.txt` SHA/versions in every release artifact.\n4. Verify `.venv/bin/python --version` in CI/launch scripts so systemd/cron never falls back to system python.",
